[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID Principles and Design Pattern Foundations"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing maintainable, scalable, and robust software requires a strong grasp of fundamental object-oriented design. The SOLID principles serve as the bedrock for modern software architecture, guiding developers on how to structure classes, interfaces, and packages. When combined with classic design patterns, these principles help eliminate technical debt and reduce architectural fragility."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have one, and only one, reason to change. This means every class should have a single job or responsibility within the software system."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High cohesion: Elements within a module belong together and serve a singular purpose."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lower coupling: Changes to one part of the system have minimal cascading effects on others."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"class ReportGenerator {
    public void generateReport(ReportData data) {
        // Generates the report content
    }
}

class ReportPrinter {
    public void printReport(Report report) {
        // Handles printing logic exclusively
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be open for extension, but closed for modification. You should be able to add new functionality without altering existing, tested code."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"interface DiscountStrategy {
    double calculateDiscount(double amount);
}

class VipDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double calculateDiscount(double amount) {
        return amount * 0.20;
    }
}

class RegularDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double calculateDiscount(double amount) {
        return amount * 0.05;
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functions that use pointers or references to base classes must be able to use objects of derived classes without knowing it. Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking application behavior."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Clients should not be forced to depend upon interfaces that they do not use. It is better to create many client-specific interfaces than one general-purpose, fat interface."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"interface Logger {
    void log(String message);
}

class DatabaseLogger implements Logger {
    public void log(String message) {
        // Write to database
    }
}

class Application {
    private final Logger logger;

    public Application(Logger logger) {
        this.logger = logger;
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting SOLID to Design Patterns"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Design patterns are practical solutions to recurring architectural problems. Most classic patterns (such as Factory, Strategy, and Observer) are direct manifestations of the SOLID principles in action, providing robust templates for scalable software engineering."}]
